1
00:00:01,238 --> 00:00:04,879
The egg sack.

2
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:12,359
Next morning, when the first light came into the sky, and the sparrows stirred

3
00:00:12,359 --> 00:00:17,398
in the trees, when the cows rattled their chains, and the rooster crowed,

4
00:00:17,398 --> 00:00:22,439
and the early automobiles went whispering along the road, Wilbur awoke

5
00:00:22,439 --> 00:00:30,439
and looked for Charlotte. He saw her up overhead in a corner near the back of his pin.

6
00:00:30,439 --> 00:00:37,559
She was very quiet. Her eight legs were spread wide. She seemed to have shrunk

7
00:00:37,559 --> 00:00:43,478
during the night. Next to her, attached to the ceiling, Wilbur saw a curious

8
00:00:43,478 --> 00:00:48,359
object. It was a sort of sack or cocoon.

9
00:00:48,359 --> 00:00:54,600
It was peachcoled and looked as though it were made of cotton candy.

10
00:00:54,600 --> 00:01:01,639
"Are you awake, Charlotte?" he said softly. "Yes," came the answer. "What is

11
00:01:01,639 --> 00:01:08,520
that nifty little thing? Did you make it? I did indeed, replied Charlotte in a

12
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:16,680
weak voice. Is it a play thing? Play thing? I should say not. It is my egg

13
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:20,760
sack. My magnum opus?

14
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:26,680
I don't know what a magnum opus is, said Wilbur. That's Latin, explained

15
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:33,799
Charlotte. It means great work. This egg sack is my great work. The finest thing

16
00:01:33,799 --> 00:01:37,398
I have ever made.

17
00:01:37,398 --> 00:01:42,279
What's inside it? asked Wilbur. Eggs.

18
00:01:42,279 --> 00:01:52,840
514 of them, she replied. 514, said Wilbur. You're kidding.

19
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:59,238
No, I'm not. I counted them. I got started counting, so I kept on just to

20
00:01:59,238 --> 00:02:02,760
keep my mind occupied.

21
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:08,760
It's a perfectly beautiful egg sack, said Wilbur, feeling as happy as though

22
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:11,559
he had constructed it himself.

23
00:02:11,559 --> 00:02:16,120
Yes, it is pretty, replied Charlotte, patting the sack with her two front

24
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:22,600
legs. Anyway, I can guarantee that it is strong. It's made out of the toughest

25
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:27,920
material I have. It's also waterproof.

26
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:32,279
The eggs are inside and will be warm and dry.

27
00:02:32,279 --> 00:02:41,478
Charlotte said Wilbur dreily. Are you really going to have 514 children?

28
00:02:41,478 --> 00:02:49,238
If nothing happens. Yes, she said. Of course, they won't show up till next spring.

29
00:02:49,238 --> 00:02:54,840
Wilbur noticed that Charlotte's voice sounded sad.

30
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:57,959
What makes you sound so downhearted?

31
00:02:57,959 --> 00:03:03,318
I should think you'd be terribly happy about this. Oh, don't pay any attention

32
00:03:03,318 --> 00:03:09,799
to me, said Charlotte. I just don't have much pep anymore. I guess I feel sad

33
00:03:09,799 --> 00:03:12,680
because I won't ever see my children.

34
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:20,760
What do you mean you won't see your children? Of course you will. We'll all see them.

35
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:27,079
It's going to be simply wonderful next spring in the barn cellar with 514

36
00:03:27,079 --> 00:03:32,120
baby spiders running around all over the place and the geese will have a new set

37
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:36,600
of gosslings and the sheep will have their new lambs.

38
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:40,120
Maybe, said Charlotte quietly.

39
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:47,398
However, I have a feeling I'm not going to see the results of last night's efforts.

40
00:03:47,398 --> 00:03:53,079
I don't feel good at all. I think I'm languishing, to tell you the truth.

41
00:03:53,079 --> 00:03:57,959
Wilbur didn't understand the word languish, and he hated to bother

42
00:03:57,959 --> 00:04:04,120
Charlotte by asking her to explain, but he was so worried he felt he had to ask.

43
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:11,639
What does languishing mean? It means I'm slowing up, feeling my age. I'm not

44
00:04:11,639 --> 00:04:20,279
young anymore, Wilbur, but I don't want you to worry about me. This is your big day today.

45
00:04:20,279 --> 00:04:26,520
Look at my web. Doesn't it show up well with the dew on it? Charlotte's web

46
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:31,879
never looked more beautiful than it looked this morning. Each strand held

47
00:04:31,879 --> 00:04:37,639
dozens of bright drops of early morning dew. The light from the east struck it

48
00:04:37,639 --> 00:04:46,040
and made it all plain and clear. It was a perfect piece of designing and building.

49
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:51,159
In another hour or two, a steady stream of people would pass by, admiring it and

50
00:04:51,159 --> 00:04:57,000
reading it, and looking at Wilbur and marveling at the miracle. As Wilbur was

51
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:04,040
studying the web, a pair of whiskers and a sharp face appeared. Slowly, Templeton

52
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:10,760
dragged himself across the pin and threw himself down in a corner.

53
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:15,000
I'm back," he said in a husky voice.

54
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:23,238
"What a night!" The rat was swollen to twice his normal size. His stomach was

55
00:05:23,238 --> 00:05:27,398
as big around as a jelly jar.

56
00:05:27,398 --> 00:05:31,559
"What a night!" he repeated horarssely.

57
00:05:31,559 --> 00:05:35,238
"What feasting and carousing!

58
00:05:35,238 --> 00:05:38,120
A real gorge!

59
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:44,359
I must have eaten the remains of 30 lunches.

60
00:05:44,359 --> 00:05:51,079
Never have I seen such leings and everything well ripened and seasoned

61
00:05:51,079 --> 00:05:57,799
with the passage of time and the heat of the day. Oh, it was rich, my friends.

62
00:05:57,799 --> 00:06:04,920
Rich. You ought to be ashamed of yourself, said Charlotte in disgust. It

63
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:10,639
would serve you right if you had an acute attack of indigestion.

64
00:06:11,079 --> 00:06:17,879
Don't worry about my stomach, snarled Templeton. It can handle anything. And

65
00:06:17,879 --> 00:06:23,478
by the way, I've got some bad news. As I came past that pig next door, the one

66
00:06:23,478 --> 00:06:29,799
that calls himself Funkle, I noticed a blue tag on the front of his pen. That

67
00:06:29,799 --> 00:06:35,559
means he has won for his prize. I guess you licked, Wilbur. You might as well

68
00:06:35,559 --> 00:06:41,398
relax. Nobody is going to hang any metal on you.

69
00:06:41,398 --> 00:06:46,279
Furthermore, I wouldn't be surprised if Zuckerman changes his mind about you.

70
00:06:46,279 --> 00:06:51,079
Wait till he gets hankering for some fresh pork and smoked ham and crisp

71
00:06:51,079 --> 00:06:56,840
bacon. He'll take the knife to you, my boy. Be still, Templeton, said

72
00:06:56,840 --> 00:07:01,398
Charlotte. You're too stuffed and bloated to know what you're saying.

73
00:07:01,398 --> 00:07:05,559
Don't pay any attention to him, Wilbur.

74
00:07:05,559 --> 00:07:10,200
Wilbur tried not to think about what the rat had just said. He decided to change

75
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:16,120
the subject. Templeton said, Wilbur, if you weren't so dopey, you would have

76
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:21,398
noticed that Charlotte has made an egg sack. She is going to become a mother.

77
00:07:21,398 --> 00:07:27,959
For your information, there are 514 eggs in that peachy little sack. Is that

78
00:07:27,959 --> 00:07:30,680
true? Asked the rat, eyeing the sack.

79
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:32,200
suspiciously.

80
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:37,238
"Yes, it's true," sighed Charlotte.

81
00:07:37,238 --> 00:07:45,879
"Congratulations," murmured Templeton. "This has been a night." He closed his eyes, pulled some

82
00:07:45,879 --> 00:07:51,799
straw over himself, and dropped off into a deep sleep. "Wilbur and Charlotte were

83
00:07:51,799 --> 00:07:57,879
glad to be rid of him for a while. At 9:00, Mr. Arabel's truck rolled into the

84
00:07:57,879 --> 00:08:04,760
fairgrounds and came to a stop at Wilbur's pen. Everybody climbed out.

85
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:11,159
Look, cried Fern. Look at Charlotte's Web. Look what it says. The grown-ups

86
00:08:11,159 --> 00:08:17,318
and the children joined hands and stood there studying the new sign. Humble,

87
00:08:17,318 --> 00:08:24,279
said Mr. Zuckerman. Now, isn't that just the word for Wilbur? Everyone rejoiced

88
00:08:24,279 --> 00:08:28,359
to find that the miracle of the web had been repeated.

89
00:08:28,359 --> 00:08:33,238
Wilbur gazed up lovingly into their faces.

90
00:08:33,238 --> 00:08:37,079
He looked very humble and very grateful.

91
00:08:37,079 --> 00:08:43,159
Fern winked at Charlotte. Lurvy soon got busy. He poured a bucket of warm slops

92
00:08:43,159 --> 00:08:47,478
into the trough. And while Wilbur ate his breakfast, Lurvy scratched him

93
00:08:47,478 --> 00:08:53,959
gently with a smooth stick. Wait a minute, cried Avery. Look at this. He

94
00:08:53,959 --> 00:08:57,318
pointed to the blue tag on Uncle's pen.

95
00:08:57,318 --> 00:09:01,639
This pig has won first prize already.

96
00:09:01,639 --> 00:09:08,120
The Zuckerman's in the arable stared at the tag. Mrs. Zuckerman began to cry.

97
00:09:08,120 --> 00:09:15,959
Nobody said a word. They just stared at the tag. Then they stared at Uncle. Then

98
00:09:15,959 --> 00:09:19,079
they stared at the tag again.

99
00:09:19,079 --> 00:09:25,559
Lurvy took out an enormous handkerchief and blew his nose very loud. So loud in

100
00:09:25,559 --> 00:09:32,318
fact that the noise was heard by stable boys over at the horse barn.

101
00:09:33,559 --> 00:09:40,279
"Can I have some money?" asked Fern. "I want to go out on the midway." "You stay

102
00:09:40,279 --> 00:09:43,559
right where you are," said her mother.

103
00:09:43,559 --> 00:09:49,478
Tears came to Fern's eyes. What's everybody crying about? asked Mr.

104
00:09:49,478 --> 00:09:55,639
Zuckerman. Let's get busy, Edith. Bring the buttermilk. Mrs. Zuckerman wiped her

105
00:09:55,639 --> 00:10:02,760
eyes with her handkerchief. She went to the truck and came back with a gallon jar of buttermilk.

106
00:10:02,760 --> 00:10:05,879
"Bath time," said Zuckerman cheerfully.

107
00:10:05,879 --> 00:10:11,959
"He and Mrs. Zuckerman and Avery climbed into Wilbur's pen. Avery slowly poured

108
00:10:11,959 --> 00:10:16,200
buttermilk on Wilbur's head and back, and as it trickled down his sides and

109
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:21,398
cheeks, Mr. and Mrs. Zuckerman rubbed it into his hair and skin.

110
00:10:21,398 --> 00:10:27,719
Passers by stopped to watch. Pretty soon, quite a crowd had gathered. Wilbur

111
00:10:27,719 --> 00:10:32,040
grew beautifully white and smooth.

112
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:36,359
The morning sun shone through his pink ears.

113
00:10:36,359 --> 00:10:42,120
He isn't as big as that pig next door, remarked one bystander. But he's

114
00:10:42,120 --> 00:10:50,040
cleaner. That's what I like. So do I, said another man. He's humble, too, said

115
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:53,079
a woman, reading the sign on the web.

116
00:10:53,079 --> 00:10:58,120
Everybody who visited the pig pin had a good word to say about Wilbur. Everyone

117
00:10:58,120 --> 00:11:04,359
admired the web. And of course, nobody noticed Charlotte.

118
00:11:04,359 --> 00:11:09,238
Suddenly, a voice was heard on the loudspeaker.

119
00:11:09,238 --> 00:11:13,398
Attention, please. It said, "Will Mr.

120
00:11:13,398 --> 00:11:18,760
Homer Zuckerman bring his famous pig to the judges booth in front of the

121
00:11:18,760 --> 00:11:25,318
grandstand? A special award will be made there in 20 minutes. Everyone is invited

122
00:11:25,318 --> 00:11:28,279
to attend. Crate your pig, please, Mr.

123
00:11:28,279 --> 00:11:33,600
Zuckerman and report to the judge's booth promptly.

124
00:11:33,799 --> 00:11:37,959
For a moment after this announcement, the arables and the Zuckermanman's were

125
00:11:37,959 --> 00:11:43,559
unable to speak or move. Then Avery picked up a handful of straw and threw

126
00:11:43,559 --> 00:11:48,680
it high in the air and gave a loud yell.

127
00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:54,120
The straw fluttered down like confetti into Fern's hair.

128
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:57,000
Mr. Zuckerman hugged Mrs. Zuckerman. Mr.

129
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:02,120
Arable kissed Mrs. Arable. Avery kissed Wilbur. Lurvy shook hands with

130
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:08,359
everybody. Fern hugged her mother. Avery hugged Fern. Mrs. Arable hugged Mrs.

131
00:12:08,359 --> 00:12:11,079
Zuckerman.

132
00:12:11,079 --> 00:12:16,359
Up overhead in the shadows of the ceiling. Charlotte crouched unseen, her

133
00:12:16,359 --> 00:12:22,520
front legs encircling her egg sack. Her heart was not beating as strongly as

134
00:12:22,520 --> 00:12:29,879
usual, and she felt weary and old, but she was sure at last that she had saved

135
00:12:29,879 --> 00:12:35,879
Wilbur's life, and she felt peaceful and contented.

136
00:12:35,879 --> 00:12:38,600
"We have no time to lose," shouted Mr.

137
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:41,000
Zuckerman. "Ly, help with the crate."

138
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,559
"Can I have some money?" asked Fern.

139
00:12:43,559 --> 00:12:49,639
"You wait," said Mrs. Arable. "Can't you see everybody's busy? Put that empty

140
00:12:49,639 --> 00:12:53,959
buttermilk jar into the truck," commanded Mr. Arable. Avery grabbed the

141
00:12:53,959 --> 00:12:57,318
jar and rushed to the truck.

142
00:12:57,318 --> 00:13:02,600
"Does my hair look all right?" asked Mrs. Zuckerman. "Looks fine," snapped

143
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:07,238
Mr. Zuckerman as he and Lurvy set the crate down in front of Wilbur. "You

144
00:13:07,238 --> 00:13:09,639
didn't even look at my hair," said Mrs.

145
00:13:09,639 --> 00:13:16,200
Zuckerman. "You're all right, Edith," said Mrs. Arable. "Just keep calm."

146
00:13:16,200 --> 00:13:21,478
Templeton, asleep in the straw, heard the commotion and awoke. He didn't know

147
00:13:21,478 --> 00:13:25,639
exactly what was going on, but when he saw the men shoving Wilbur into the

148
00:13:25,639 --> 00:13:28,439
crate, he made up his mind to go along.

149
00:13:28,439 --> 00:13:32,760
He watched his chance, and when no one was looking, he crept into the crate and

150
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:36,920
buried himself in the straw at the bottom.

151
00:13:36,920 --> 00:13:39,959
"All righty, boys!" cried Mr. Zuckerman.

152
00:13:39,959 --> 00:13:46,600
"Let's go!" He and Mr. Arable and Lurvy and Avery grabbed the crate and boosted

153
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:52,279
it over the side of the pen and up into the truck. Fern jumped aboard and sat on

154
00:13:52,279 --> 00:13:59,719
top of the crate. She still had straw in her hair and looked very pretty and excited.

155
00:13:59,719 --> 00:14:04,600
Mr. Rarable started the motor. Everyone climbed in and off they drove to the

156
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:07,159
judges booth in front of the grandstand.

157
00:14:07,159 --> 00:14:12,200
As they passed the ferris wheel, Fern gazed up at it and wished she were in

158
00:14:12,200 --> 00:14:18,079
the topmost car with Henry Fussy at her side.

159
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:20,000
Chapter 20. The hour of triumph.
